When onlookers accused Paul of reviling the high priest, the apostle immediately admitted his error, saying: “Brothers, I did not know he was high priest. For it is written, ‘ You must not speak injuriously of a ruler of your people. ’ ” — Acts 23: 1 - 5.
What Paul had said — that the one appointed as judge should not resort to violence — was valid.
Still, he apologized for unknowingly speaking to the high priest in a manner that could be viewed as being disrespectful. * Paul’s apology paved the way for the Sanhedrin to listen to what he had to say.
When the apostle Paul noticed that the Christians in Corinth were taking fellow servants of God to secular courts over such personal differences as financial disagreements, he corrected them: “Why do you not rather let yourselves be wronged?
