The Search for Happiness
A FEW years ago, people in France, Germany, Great Britain, and the United States were asked, “What does it take to be happy? ”
Of those interviewed, 89 percent said that it takes good health; 79 percent mentioned a satisfying marriage or partnership; 62 percent pointed to the rewards of parenthood; and 51 percent thought a successful career was needed for happiness.
Are all such individuals doomed to a life of unhappiness? And would the assumed happiness of those who presently enjoy good health and a satisfying marriage vanish if their situation changed?
Are We Searching in the Right Places?
Could it be, though, that some are trying too hard to find happiness?
Trying to become rich; striving for fame or recognition; pursuing political, social, or economic goals; or simply living for self and instant gratification all fail to deliver happiness.
