Walk in the Way of Integrity
WHEN Satan rebelled in the garden of Eden, he raised the universal issue of the rightfulness of God’s sovereignty over all His creatures. Some time later, he challenged that humans would serve God only as long as doing so was to their advantage.
Thus, man’s integrity has become an important part of the issue of Jehovah’s universal sovereignty.
While God’s sovereignty is not dependent on the integrity of his creatures, humans and spirit sons of God can demonstrate where they stand on this issue.
With confidence Job said: “ will weigh me in accurate scales and God will get to know my integrity. ”
Integrity is what King David of ancient Israel asked Jehovah to inspect when he prayed: “Judge me, O Jehovah, for I myself have walked in my own integrity, and in Jehovah I have trusted, that I may not wobble. ”
How vital that we too walk in the way of integrity!
But what is integrity, and what does it mean to walk in its way?
What will help us stay on the course of integrity?
It is moral soundness or completeness of heart devotion to God. Satan questioned Job’s motives when he told God: “For a change, thrust out your hand, please, and touch as far as bone and his flesh and see whether he will not curse you to your very face. ”
King David was imperfect and made several serious mistakes during his life.
What does walking in integrity involve?
When we bear up under trials, remain firm despite adversities, or resist temptations from the ungodly world, our integrity becomes evident.
What do you learn from David’s plea that Jehovah examine his kidneys and his heart?
The kidneys are positioned deep within the body.
In what way does Jehovah refine our figurative kidneys and heart?
David pleaded that his kidneys and his heart be refined. How does Jehovah refine what we are on the inside?
David sang: “I shall bless Jehovah, who has given me advice.
It means that divine counsel reached the deepest parts of David’s being and settled there, correcting his innermost thoughts and emotions.
What helped David to walk in God’s truth?
What can help us to walk in the way of integrity?
We have the example of Moses, who rejected the glories of Egypt. Keeping in mind the endurance of Job will undoubtedly help us to strengthen our resolve to remain loyal to Jehovah despite illnesses and misfortunes.
What if we become victims of persecution? Why, recalling Daniel’s experience in the lions ’ pit would infuse us with courage! — Daniel 6: 16 - 22.
What kind of associations should we avoid?
Referring to yet another factor that fortified his integrity, David said: “I have not sat with men of untruth; and with those who hide what they are I do not come in. I have hated the congregation of evildoers, and with the wicked ones I do not sit. ”
Do we refuse to sit with men of untruth through television programs, videos, motion pictures, Internet sites, or other means?
Do we stay away from those who hide what they are?
Some at school or at our place of employment may feign friendship with us for devious purposes.
Do we really want to develop close ties with those who do not walk in God’s truth? Behind claims of sincerity, apostates may also hide their intent to draw us away from serving Jehovah.
