The Need to Be Long - Suffering
Paul wrote: “Love is long - suffering. ”
The Greek expression translated “long - suffering ” denotes patience and slowness to anger.
Children seldom ask just once for some desired object.
King Solomon wrote: “The insight of a man certainly slows down his anger. ” Parents gain insight into their children’s behavior by recalling that at one time they too used to “speak as a babe, to think as a babe, to reason as a babe. ”
Parents, can you remember bothering your mother or father to give in to a childish request?
It carries the idea of training and education. What is the purpose of such discipline?
