What is involved in disciplining children in a kind and loving manner?
Or, as an alternative, they might allow their children to suggest an hour and to offer reasons for their preference. The parents may then state their own desired time and explain why they feel that this is appropriate.
If there is a difference of opinion, as there will likely be, what then?
In some cases, the parents may decide that it might be possible to accommodate their children’s wishes when Bible principles are not jeopardized. Does this mean that the parents are abdicating, or giving up, their authority?
To answer that question, consider the way in which Jehovah lovingly exercised his authority when dealing with Lot and his family. After escorting Lot, his wife, and his daughters out of Sodom, the angels said to them: “Escape to the mountainous region for fear you may be swept away! ”
Lot then suggested an alternative: “Please, now, this city is nearby to flee there and it is a small thing.
May I, please, escape there? ”
Certainly not!
Nevertheless, he considered Lot’s request and chose to extend extra kindness to him in this matter. If you are a parent, are there times when you can consider your children’s concerns when establishing family rules?
How can parents imitate Jehovah when training their children? If it is to be kind, the punishment and the way it is administered need to be tailored to the child.
Parents, do you know your children’s strengths and weaknesses?
Are you able to use that knowledge in a positive, kind way to train them?
If so, you are proving that you love your children.
