Highlights From the Books of Joel and Amos
ALL he tells about himself is that he is “Joel the son of Pethuel. ” In the book bearing his name, Joel says so little about matters other than his message that even the time of his prophecy can only be estimated — about 820 B.C.E., nine years after Uzziah became king over Judah.
The likely reason is that he wants to emphasize the message and not the messenger.
The invaders are referred to as “a people numerous and mighty ” and as“ powerful men. ”
Before the coming of his great day, though, Jehovah will “pour out spirit on every sort of flesh ” and will“ give portents in the heavens and on the earth. ” — Joel 2: 12, 18 - 20, 28 - 31. The nations are challenged: “Beat your plowshares into swords and your pruning shears into lances ” and prepare for war.
The day of Jehovah is the time of the execution of Jehovah’s judgment upon his enemies, resulting in destruction for them but salvation for true worshippers.
There is no record in the Bible of an insect invasion of the land of Canaan of the magnitude described in the book of Joel. Therefore, the assault Joel describes is apparently pictorial of the time in 33 C.E. when Jehovah started to pour out his spirit upon early followers of Christ and they began preaching the message that tormented false religious leaders.
In the days of Judean King Jehoshaphat, whose name means “Jehovah Is Judge, ” God delivered Judah from surrounding nations by confusing their military forces.
Only he “who calls on the name of Jehovah will get away safe ” on“ the great and fear - inspiring day of Jehovah. ” How grateful we can be that Jehovah pours out his spirit on every sort of flesh and has young and old, male and female, share in the work of prophesying, that is, declaring “the magnificent things of God ”!
Amos warns that Jehovah will “hold an accounting against the altars of Bethel ” and“ will strike down the winter house in addition to the summer house. ” — Amos 3: 14, 15.
Used in parallel with “want of bread, ” the phrase could refer to a time of famine when teeth remain clean because of the lack of food.
It likely refers to the tax levied by the king for the upkeep of his horsemen and animals. This tax had to be paid “at the start of the coming up of the later sowing. ”
Before they could do that, however, a locust swarm formed and devoured their crops along with the other vegetation.
Summer fruit is picked toward the end of the harvest season, that is, toward the end of the agricultural year. When Jehovah caused Amos to see “a basket of summer fruit, ” it meant that the end was near for Israel.
We should not discourage hardworking pioneers, traveling overseers, missionaries, or members of the Bethel family by urging them to give up their full - time service for a so - called normal way of life.
Godly fear should move us to be zealous preachers of the Kingdom message.
The situation of a man doing so is like that of someone who flees from a lion only to encounter a bear and then runs away from the bear only to be bitten by a serpent. We would be wise to “keep awake ” spiritually and to remain in a state of readiness. — Luke 21: 36.
We should be fearless and bold about declaring God’s message.
What We Should Do
Pursuing a Meaningful Purpose in Life
To have true purpose in life is to have a reason for living, a clear objective, and a focal point for our efforts.
The fact that we are endowed with intelligence, conscience, and the ability to reason implies that the Creator had a good purpose in putting us here. Logically, therefore, we can find and fulfill our true purpose only by living in harmony with the Creator’s purpose.
What Is Your Purpose in Life?
Regrettably, many people focus their life almost exclusively on the acquiring of material things.
A recent survey of millionaires in Asia reveals that many of them “feel insecure and troubled, even as they enjoy the social status and the sense of accomplishment their wealth brings. ” — Ecclesiastes 5: 11.
Jesus spoke of “the deceptive power of riches. ” How are riches deceptive?
How may we “get a firm hold on the real life ”?
To most people, the expression “the real life ” conjures up an image of luxury and pleasure. One Asian news magazine notes: “Those who watch films or TV learn to desire what they see, to dream of what they might have. ”
When will faithful ones enjoy “the real life ”? How sad if one entertains no hope beyond life in this present world!
At that time, Christians can enjoy “the real life, ” that is,“ everlasting life ” in perfection, either in heaven or under the loving rulership of God’s Kingdom government! — 1 Timothy 6: 12.
